        "detailed_analysis": "The FFT-based metrology returns a square, essentially cubic projected cell: a1 = 0.403 nm, a2 = 0.4005 nm, gamma = 90.0 deg (lattice_constant 0.4017 \u00b1 0.0077 nm). This is the perovskite ABO3 pseudocubic {100} spacing and matches both the LSAT substrate (a \u2248 0.3868 nm) and the LLTO film (pseudocubic a \u2248 0.386\u20130.39 nm) to within the ~3\u20134% calibration uncertainty expected for a single-frame FOV-derived pixel size \u2014 i.e., the measured ~0.40 nm is consistent with a perovskite lattice viewed down a <100> zone axis, with the absolute value biased slightly high by calibration. The reciprocal lattice is internally self-consistent: the (1,0)/(0,1) fundamentals at 0.4015 nm, their {110} at 0.284 nm, and {200} at 0.2008 nm form a clean centered-square census with explained_fraction = 0.86 and 44 indexed reflections, single-lattice (multi_lattice = false). The reported nn_distance of 0.2842 nm is NOT a lattice constant \u2014 it is the bright-column-to-bright-column {110} projected spacing (= a/sqrt2), because a {110}-type reflection is resolved (centered_sublattice = true). The lattice constant proper is the ~0.401 nm fundamental; these two quantities must be kept distinct.\n\nCritically, the 'strongest satellite' at 0.4015 nm flagged as a candidate is a spacing-relation artifact, not a physical superstructure: 0.4015 nm IS the fundamental {100} spacing, and the tool only labeled it a 'satellite' because it is 2x the 0.2008 nm {200} reflection \u2014 the {200} is the harmonic of {100}, not the other way around. The superstructure discriminator confirms this reading: spot_snr_domain (55.9) is only marginally above bulk (48.0), superstructure_domain_fraction = 0.0, and the verdict is explicitly 'NOT confirmed as a localized ordered domain (ambiguous / possible edge artifact).' There is therefore no evidence for a resolved vacancy-ordered or cation-ordered superlattice domain in this frame \u2014 consistent with, but not proof of, a disordered (non-superstructured) LLTO/substrate region within the field of view. The row-intensity profile shows a strong smooth ramp/step in the lower band (step_amplitude ~2935, ramp_span ~3212), correctly attributed to shading / thickness gradient rather than a per-column Z-contrast interface step; the column profile shows no clear step (step_amplitude ~270, within shading/noise). The bright bottom band is a specimen-thickness/shading gradient and should not be read as the film/substrate interface.",
